      'The KYC rule requires broker-dealers to use "reasonable diligence" to collect and maintain essential information on their customers. The rule requires firms to check:\n\nCustomers\' identities\n\n• Investment histories\n• Investment objectives\n\nSources of funds This information is used to ensure that customers are not laundering money. It also is used in making suitable recommendations for the customers\' accounts. If the customer does not provide all the information required on a new account form, the account may still be opened if the firm collects certain essential facts.\n\nThese include facts necessary to:\n\n• Effectively service a customer\'s account • Carry out special handling instructions for the account • Understand who has trading authority on the account • Comply with securities laws, regulations, and rules\n• Effectively service a customer\'s account\n• Carry out special handling instructions for the account\n\nUnderstand who has trading authority on the account\n\n• Comply with securities laws, regulations, and rules\n\nWhen information on the new account form is missing, a supervisor must be informed, and inquiries should be made and documented as to why the missing information was not provided. The supervisor should make sure any of the missing information is not an essential fact. The KYC rule also requires that the firm make regular contact with customers to check if investment objectives or essential facts have changed.\n\nWhile the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) requires broker-dealers to update suitability information at least every three years, the KYC obligation may require broker-dealers to update their customers\' information more often.',
