      'Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Requirements',
      'Money laundering has three phases:\n\n• Placement: The initial entry of illicit cash into the financial system. This is often done by making cash deposits into a financial institution below reporting thresholds; purchasing gambling chips; or repaying loans with cash, etc.\n\n• Layering: Once cash has been deposited, the funds are often wired to a series of other financial institutions that make it hard to trace the funds back to their original source. Integration: The final stage of money laundering, where the funds are returned to the criminal from what appear to be legitimate sources.\n\nSuspicious Activity Reports (SARs) The Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) requires broker-dealers to file SARs (Suspicious Activity Reports) with FinCEN (Financial Crimes Enforcement Network, part of the Department of Treasury), which acts as the central collection point for these reports. If potential money laundering or violations of the BSA are detected or suspected, a report is required to be filed within 30 days.',
